# Exporting Variables

A module keeps everything to itself unless told otherwise. To make a value visible to other files, you **export** it. A module can export as many values as it likes, of any kind: functions, constants, objects, numbers.

There are two ways to write it, and they mean the same thing.

## Form One: `export` Before the Declaration

Put the keyword in front of the declaration:

```javascript
export function fahrenheitToCelsius(temp) {
  return (temp - 32) * 5 / 9;
}

export const freezingPointF = 32;

export let readingCount = 0;
```

It works with `function`, `const`, and `let` alike.

Here is the video's `weather-utils.js`, exporting two conversion functions. The cell runs, so you can check the conversions yourself:

```{javascript autorun}
// weather-utils.js

export function fahrenheitToCelsius(temp) {
  return (temp - 32) * 5 / 9;
}

export function celsiusToFahrenheit(temp) {
  return temp * 9 / 5 + 32;
}

console.log('0C is ' + celsiusToFahrenheit(0) + 'F');
console.log('100C is ' + celsiusToFahrenheit(100) + 'F');
console.log('212F is ' + fahrenheitToCelsius(212) + 'C');
```

In a real project the calls at the bottom would live in another file, `main.js`, which imports what it needs:

```javascript
// main.js
import { celsiusToFahrenheit } from './weather-utils.js';

console.log(celsiusToFahrenheit(0));
```

```html
<script type="module" src="main.js"></script>
```

Note what happens if you write that import and forget the `export`: it does not work. There is nothing to import, because a module shares nothing by default.

## Form Two: One `export` at the End

You can also declare everything normally and list the exports in a single statement, conventionally at the bottom of the file, with the names inside curly braces:

```{javascript autorun}
// simple-math.js

function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

function subtract(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}

const PI = 3.14;

export { add, subtract, PI };

console.log(add(2, 3));
```

That is exactly equivalent to writing `export` in front of all three declarations. Which form to use is a matter of taste: the keyword-in-front version keeps the information next to the thing it describes, and the list-at-the-end version gives you one place to see a module's whole public surface.

Watch the punctuation, because it is a common slip. The names go in **curly braces**, separated by commas:

```javascript
export { add, subtract };     // correct
```

Not brackets, not parentheses, and not a bare list.

## Anything Unexported Stays Private

This is the part that matters most. Exporting is opt-in, one value at a time.

```{javascript autorun}
// temperature-report.js

function roundTo1(value) {
  return Math.round(value * 10) / 10;
}

export function report(tempC) {
  return roundTo1(tempC * 9 / 5 + 32) + 'F';
}

console.log(report(21.6666));
```

`report` is exported and `roundTo1` is not, so another file can call `report` and cannot see `roundTo1` at all. That is a feature: it means you can rename or rewrite `roundTo1` freely, knowing nothing outside this file could have depended on it.

If a file has ten functions and you export two, the other eight are private. Not hidden-but-reachable, not global, simply not available.

## Exercises

1. Export both of these, using the keyword in front of each declaration.

    ```{javascript}
    const apiBaseUrl = '/api';

    function buildUrl(path) {
      return apiBaseUrl + path;
    }
    ```
    ```+solution
    export const apiBaseUrl = '/api';

    export function buildUrl(path) {
      return apiBaseUrl + path;
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && exports.apiBaseUrl === '/api').should('equal', true).run('Put `export` in front of the `const` declaration', 'Exported the constant');
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && typeof exports.buildUrl === 'function').should('equal', true).run('Put `export` in front of the function declaration too', 'Exported the function');
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && typeof exports.buildUrl === 'function' && exports.buildUrl('/users') === '/api/users').should('equal', true).run('The exported function should still work, returning `/api/users` for `/users`', 'The exported function works');
    ```

2. Export `makeLabel` and `separator` with a single statement at the end of the file. Leave `trim` private.

    ```{javascript}
    const separator = ': ';

    function trim(text) {
      return text.trim();
    }

    function makeLabel(name) {
      return 'Label' + separator + trim(name);
    }

    // Write your export statement here
    ```
    ```+solution
    const separator = ': ';

    function trim(text) {
      return text.trim();
    }

    function makeLabel(name) {
      return 'Label' + separator + trim(name);
    }

    export { makeLabel, separator };
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/export\s*\{[\s\S]*\}/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Use the list form: `export { ... };` with the names in curly braces', 'Used the end-of-file form');
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && typeof exports.makeLabel === 'function').should('equal', true).run('`makeLabel` should be one of the exported names', 'Exported makeLabel');
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && exports.separator === ': ').should('equal', true).run('`separator` should be exported as well', 'Exported separator');
    wrap(typeof exports !== 'undefined' && typeof exports.trim === 'undefined').should('equal', true).run('Leave `trim` out of the export list so it stays private to this file', 'Kept trim private');
    ```

3. You want to export `add` and `subtract` in one statement at the end of the file. Which line is correct?

    ```{mcq}
    Pick the correct end-of-file export syntax.
    correct: Correct! The names go inside curly braces, separated by commas.
    wrong: Look again at the punctuation in the `simple-math.js` cell.
    [x] `export { add, subtract };`
    [ ] `export add, subtract;`
    [ ] `export [add, subtract];`
    [ ] `export (add, subtract);`
    ```

4. `mathUtils.js` has ten helper functions, and you put `export` in front of two of them. What about the other eight?

    ```{mcq}
    What happens to the functions you did not export?
    correct: Correct! They stay private to `mathUtils.js`. No other file can reach them, which is exactly why you are free to change them later.
    wrong: Remember that a module shares nothing unless you say so.
    [x] They remain private to `mathUtils.js` and no other file can use them
    [ ] They are exported automatically, just without names
    [ ] The code fails, because a module has to export everything it declares
    [ ] They become global variables usable anywhere without importing
    ```

